2. Application Areas and Industry Use Cases
2.1 Clinical Decision Support Systems (CDSS) for Healthcare Providers
Use Case: Pediatricians and psychiatrists often struggle with ambiguous behavioral symptoms in toddlers, leading to high misdiagnosis rates or delayed confirmation
. The "Dual-Shield System" acts as an objective, Tier-2 secondary opinion. By uploading a standard peripheral blood microarray to a Flask/FastAPI backend, doctors receive a real-time transcriptomic probability score to confidently rule in or rule out ASD, drastically cutting down the diagnostic wait time from years to days
.
2.2 Digital Phenotyping and Telehealth Triage
Use Case: Utilizing compact subsets of behavioral screening items (discovered via ML tools like Recursive Feature Elimination), mobile health application developers can create highly efficient, less burdensome screening apps for parents
. Because the algorithm proves that markers like "eye contact", "pretend play", and "gaze following" hold the highest predictive weights, engineers can design targeted digital phenotyping tools (using device cameras and computer vision) to automatically quantify these specific constructs outside the clinic
.
2.3 Bioinformatics and Biomarker Discovery (Pharmaceuticals)
Use Case: The ML pipeline functions as a powerful, unbiased research engine for identifying novel drug targets. Because models like L1-Regularized Logistic Regression and GWO-SVM aggressively filter out noise to isolate "sparse signatures", bioinformaticians can extract these specific non-zero genes to identify the core systemic dysfunctions of ASD
. For instance, mapping the selected features revealed that Cholesterol Biosynthesis and Lipid Metabolism act as hub pathways in ASD pathophysiology, giving pharmaceutical companies concrete targets for metabolic or immune-based therapeutics
.
2.4 Precision Medicine & Subgroup Stratification
Use Case: Autism is clinically heterogeneous, meaning a "one-size-fits-all" treatment strategy often fails
. By using unsupervised machine learning (e.g., Partitioning Around Medoids (PAM) clustering on Random Forest proximity matrices) over gene expression profiles, clinical researchers can stratify patients into homogeneous biological subgroups
. For example, classifying patients based on the transcriptomic likelihood of developing language impairments allows therapists to proactively match early interventions and specialized educational resources tailored specifically to that child's predicted trajectory
.